<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<window>

    <defaultcontrol always="true">300</defaultcontrol>

    <!-- SKINMOD: park focus off the edit control when the keyboard closes over Search (1185).
         Kodi restores focus to whatever opened the dialog, which here is edit control 9099. An
         edit control changes how keys are routed: CInputManager sets useKeyboard=true whenever
         the focused control is GUICONTROL_EDIT, which sends the key through the VirtualKeyboard
         keymap section instead of the global one, and there backspace maps to Backspace rather
         than Back. CGUIEditControl::OnAction then returns true for ACTION_BACKSPACE
         unconditionally - even with an empty field and the cursor at 0 - so the action is
         swallowed and never falls through to the global Back binding. Result: the window cannot
         be left. Escape still works because it is absent from the VirtualKeyboard section and
         falls back to PreviousMenu, which is on the pass-through allowlist and is not consumed.
         Remotes that report Back as a backspace key (Android TV, Fire TV, most CEC) hit the
         swallowed path, which is why Back is dead on a remote but Escape works on a PC.

         An <onback> on 9099 does NOT fix this: the action arriving is ACTION_BACKSPACE, which is
         consumed before CGUIControl::OnAction ever reaches its ACTION_NAV_BACK case. The only
         fix is to not leave focus on an edit control, so focus is parked on 9090, the offscreen
         idle button, which is a plain button and routes keys normally.

         Here rather than on Cancel (301) because 301 is only one of the ways out: Back or Escape
         on the keyboard itself closes the dialog without clicking any button, and that route
         lands in exactly the same trap. onunload covers every route.

         Alarm rather than a direct SetFocus: during deinit the dialog is still the active window,
         so the builtin would resolve against the keyboard and miss. The deferral lets it land on
         1185, the same mechanism control 300 already uses. Its own SetFocus(5000) alarms are
         timed to fire after this one so the results win when there are results to focus. -->
    <onunload condition="Window.IsVisible(1185)">AlarmClock(SearchKeyboardPark,SetFocus(9090),00:01,silent)</onunload>

    <controls>
        <include>Keyboard_Standard</include>
    </controls>
</window>
